WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.415
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.400
MOLINA: Good afternoon.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.460
Welcome to the 21st annual
Victor P. Starr Memorial

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.110
Lecture.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.210
As you know, we have
had these lectures

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.130
for over two decades in
these departments in honor

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.610
Victor Starr who was a faculty
in our department, actually

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.510
was the department of
meteorology when he was there.

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.900
And these lectures are
on broad ranging topics

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.860
in atmospheric sciences.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.450
So we're very pleased to have
today the first such lecture

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.100
in atmospheric chemistry.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.170
And we're particularly
pleased that it's

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:58.450
Professor Paul Crutzen who's
giving the presentation today.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.780
Paul is a close colleague
and friend of mine.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.036
And I think it's very
fitting that he will give us

00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:08.320
this first atmospheric
chemistry lecture, because Paul

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.930
started as a meteorologist.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:15.020
But eventually he saw the light
and became a chemist as well.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.330
So let me tell you a
few things about Paul.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.920
He was born in Amsterdam
in the Netherlands.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.940
And what he studied
first I believe

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.940
was engineering in Amsterdam.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:32.350
In fact, his first jobs
were as an engineer

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.150
in the bridge
construction bureau

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.130
of the city of Amsterdam.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.110
Then eventually
he moved to Sweden

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.930
where he was working at the
house construction bureau.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.030
But eventually he got
a PhD in meteorology

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.640
at the University of
Stockholm in Sweden.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.960
I think he started there
with the job as a computer

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.940
programmer, but
switched to meteorology.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.210
Between 1974 and 1980, he
came to the United States

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:12.217
where he was the director of the
air quality division at NCAR.

00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:14.050
I think we call it the
atmospheric chemistry

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.340
division at the National Center
for Atmospheric Research.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:24.530
And in 1980, he
went back to Europe,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.670
to Germany, where he
has been there until now

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.690
as a director of the
atmospheric chemistry

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:34.690
division of the Max Planck
Institute [INAUDIBLE] in Mainz.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:38.200
At the same time he
has been and is still

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.660
a part-time professor at
the Scripps Institution

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:45.880
of Oceanography in San Diego
as well in Utrecht University

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.140
in the Netherlands.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.210
Now, Paul has many honors and
awards, several pages, which

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:54.640
I won't take the time to read.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:58.060
But I thought I'd select just
a handful, with a certain bias,

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.950
as you will see.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.320
One of them is he
is an AUG fellow.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.410
He became a fellow of the
American Geophysical Union

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.150
in 1986.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.710
I just became a fellow myself
just a year or two ago.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.240
In 1989, he got the Tyler
Prize for the environment.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.550
It happens to be a prize I also
received at some point in time.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.240
Another very interesting
prize is the recipient

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.250
of the Max Planck
Forschungspreis, which

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:36.850
is a program that is given
to a German investigator

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.730
in collaboration with
an American scientist.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:44.170
So I was fortunate to
receive that with Paul.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.210
In fact, it was a
very productive prize,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.550
because we got the
funding to do research,

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.620
and we wonderful
student, Thomas Coop who

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.570
was Paul's student to begin
with and became my student

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:56.970
afterwards.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:02.647
And then in 1995, Paul received
the Nobel Prize in chemistry.

00:04:02.647 --> 00:04:04.480
And of course, I had
the honor of sharing it

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.690
with him and with
Sherwood Rowland.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.643
So these are the only
prizes I will mention.

00:04:11.643 --> 00:04:13.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.019
But let me do one
additional thing.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:20.380
I want to read you the
title of his PhD theses

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.510
in 1968 that he got at the
University of Stockholm.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.330
And it is "The termination
of parameters appearing

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:32.590
in the dry and the wet
photochemical theories of ozone

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.120
in the stratosphere."

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.170
So that was in 1968.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.840
So this really brings
me to explain to you

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.840
that this groundbreaking
work in understanding

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:47.050
how the stratosphere
functions, how

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:49.330
the chemistry of the
stratosphere works,

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.110
really discovering the
catalytic cycles, the nature

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.780
of these cycles is very
important in explaining how we

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.900
see that trace species, in
this case nitrogen oxides

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.410
or hydrogen oxides, very small
amounts of certain catalysts

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:05.900
can have this very large effect
i controlling the abundance

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.550
of ozone in the stratosphere.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:12.190
And also in Stockholm,
Paul had a second thesis.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.090
I think it's a doctorate
in science thesis.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.140
This was in 1973.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.270
And the title is "On
the photochemistry

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.450
of ozone in the
stratosphere and troposphere

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.040
and pollution of
the stratosphere

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.580
by high flying aircraft."

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.040
So this is another milestone.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:30.300
Paul was really the
first one to recognize

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.680
that human activities
could impact ozone

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:38.370
in the stratosphere, which
is this major natural system,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.500
that's truly a global issue.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.970
So it all goes back to
his early days in Sweden.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.440
Since then he has made
numerous contributions, both

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.180
to stratospheric
chemistry, as well

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.205
as to tropospheric chemistry,
again too many for me to list.

00:05:56.205 --> 00:06:00.840
But he will talk to us today
about tropospheric chemistry.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.420
The title of his presentation
is "The Major Role

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.030
of the Tropics in Atmospheric
Chemistry, the Need

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.650
for Strong Research Efforts."

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.416
I give you Paul Crutzen.

00:06:10.416 --> 00:06:13.832
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:22.540
CRUTZEN: Well, thank you
for this introduction.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:26.330
Now, you took 10
minutes of my talk.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.830
[LAUGHTER] So I have
to shorten it down

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:33.683
I guess because everybody wants
to go to the drinks afterwards.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.840
Well, it's nice to
be here and in fact

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.570
to have such an audience
so late in the evening,

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.060
because normally this
time I go to bed.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:55.220
Well, my lecture will not
be really for the experts.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:01.960
So this will be a little
bit of a fundamental lecture

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.540
on how the chemistry of
the atmosphere works.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.630
So if the experts already
know that and they know it,

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:08.320
they can leave.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.370
But you're also allowed to stay.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.200
If you look at the distribution
of ozone in the atmosphere,

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.390
and we all know that
almost everything

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:21.300
is in the stratosphere.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.160
And about the stratosphere
I will not speak very much

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.620
at this lecture.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.810
There's very little
ozone in the troposphere.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.580
At most, 10% of all
atmospheric ozone

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.750
is located in the troposphere.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:43.270
And for a long time, it was
thought that ozone was not

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:44.760
very interesting.

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.720
And some meteorologists
had been looking at it.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.060
It was normally
believed that ozone

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.980
would come down from
the stratosphere

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:55.930
into the troposphere
and would be

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.890
destroyed at the Earth surface.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:06.670
This was basically the
belief, say, around 1970.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:10.270
And in fact, ozone is
of course coming down

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:14.470
from the stratosphere
and the troposphere.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:21.580
This is the picture which was
prevalent in the early days.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.560
Ozone was produced
in the stratosphere.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.040
And a very small
fraction of it--

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.890
actually the flux of ozone
from the stratosphere

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:33.580
into the troposphere
is less than 1%

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:36.280
of all ozone formed
in the stratosphere.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.190
So a little small
leakage will bring ozone

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:40.960
into the troposphere.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:43.330
But it was believed
for a very long time,

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:45.782
that that was the whole
story, that it was then

00:08:45.782 --> 00:08:46.990
they destroyed at the ground.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:55.810
And that this happens
is also sometimes

00:08:55.810 --> 00:08:58.130
very clearly observed.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:01.630
Here, we have a
so-called intrusion

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:04.330
of material from
the stratosphere

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:06.040
into the troposphere.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:12.310
We see here isolines of
radioactive species, which

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:16.540
in the '50s was produced
copiously in the stratosphere

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:18.880
by bomb testing.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.440
We see ozone, and
we see something

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:29.620
in meteorologically stable
or almost inert capacity,

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:33.020
namely, the potential vorticity.

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:36.790
And you see all these isolines
going into the troposphere.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:38.860
And part of this ozone,
which follows that

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.490
is then also staying
in the troposphere.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.760
So the picture looked
extremely clear.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:49.180
And in fact, very
little interest

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:53.300
was devoted to ozone
in the troposphere

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:56.740
until roughly the
middle of the '70s.

00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:00.140
We all know how
important ozone is.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:06.130
The stratospheric ozone filters
out basically all radiation

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:13.840
between 240 and 300 nanometer
which is coming down

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.730
on the Earth from the sun.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:21.040
However, this filtering
is not perfect.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.370
Around 300 nanometer
UV radiation

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:27.850
comes down to the surface,
very much dependent

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:31.810
on the amount of ozone
in the stratosphere.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.390
And this radiation is harmful.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:39.280
Too much of it, we all know
it in summertime, sun burns.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:42.760
Too much of it is
not good for you.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.970
So that is normally the
UV radiation is considered

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.550
to be a negative effect.

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:52.390
Also, ozone in the troposphere
is considered to be negative.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:56.410
It harms people.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.400
Lungs can be damaged and so on.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.300
And plant life can be
damaged by too much ozone

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.360
in the troposphere.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:07.120
However it turns out that
this ozone in the troposphere

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.620
has also very important, let's
call it, positive consequences.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:15.770
And one of the reasons is that--

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:19.960
and it goes back to some
simple reactions here--

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:23.890
ozone photolysis at
wavelength below--

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:28.240
nowadays we say
almost 400 nanometer--

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.110
below in this wavelength
region produces O atoms

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.890
in an electronically sized
state, O single D, which

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.500
then has enough energy
to react with water vapor

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:43.600
to make hydroxyl radicals,
hence these hydroxyl radicals,

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.420
which play an enormously
important role

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:51.340
in keeping the atmosphere clean,
because the hydroxyl radicals

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.850
react with almost
all species, which

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:58.990
are produced by
nature or by mankind

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.600
and deposited in the atmosphere.

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:08.260
So if we summarize the
two reactions again--

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:11.620
by the way, you see here that
because of these two reactions,

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.660
ozone is destroyed.

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:17.020
We come back to that, to budget
of ozone in the troposphere.

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:21.730
And we see here a number of
species which react with OH,

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:23.740
like methane.

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:28.330
Methane reacts rather slowly
with the hydroxyl radical.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:31.870
And it has a lifetime on
average around the world

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.100
of about 8 years.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:37.600
And that means that methane
is found both in the northern

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:43.390
and in the southern hemisphere,
although almost all methane is

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:47.410
produced in the
northern hemisphere.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:50.710
But we go to other species
like ethane reacts much faster

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:52.120
with OH.

00:12:52.120 --> 00:12:55.460
And we see definitely much
more in the northern hemisphere

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.070
than in the southern hemisphere.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:13:04.030
We go to propane, lifetime
of a few days, 10 days.

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:06.040
And we hardly find
anything of that

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.930
in the southern hemisphere.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:10.120
And of course,
isoprene, which is

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:13.660
emitted by the
deciduous forests,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.690
has such a short
lifetime, orders of hours,

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:22.360
that we only find this compound
close to the forest regions.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.850
We have a number of sulfur,
chlorine, and many other gases,

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:29.430
which are removed from the
atmosphere by reaction with OH.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:33.640
And this was a major discovery,
which goes back to 1971

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:41.900
by Chip Levy, who worked here
at the Smithsonian Institute,

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:44.680
just down the river almost.

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:49.780
Before this paper and
before this knowledge,

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.330
it will simply not
know how compounds

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:57.750
which go into the atmosphere,
how they are removed.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:04.410
And I show you later
some funny estimates

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.930
about budgets of
trace gases, which

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:12.800
were produced at that time,
before that time, because they

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.690
were far out from reality.

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:19.140
Now, in the lowest figures,
the lowest two figures here,

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:27.400
I sort of summarize the good
and the bad of UV radiation.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:29.290
Here, we have the
positive factor.

00:14:29.290 --> 00:14:34.740
If we multiply the efficiency
with which O single

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.810
D atoms are produced by
ultraviolet radiation,

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:41.860
multiply that with the flux
of ultraviolet radiation

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:43.950
down to the Earth's
surface, which

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:47.620
is a strong function of ozone
going down to its shorter

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:50.260
wavelength, we get
curves which look

00:14:50.260 --> 00:14:52.320
like this, dependent
on the amount of ozone

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.130
in the stratosphere.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.730
That's the positive part.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.590
But if you look
at the DNA damage,

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:02.690
we get a curve which
looks very similar.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:09.160
So you cannot say that UV
radiation is only damaging.

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.370
It has positive, it
has negative sides.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.460
In the '70s, it
was also realized

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:22.950
that the ozone in the
troposphere was not inert.

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:25.470
Of course, we saw
already one reaction

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:30.420
in which ozone is
destroyed, the photolysis

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:32.910
by ultraviolet radiation.

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:37.200
But also reactions
were discovered

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.320
in which ozone is either
produced or destroyed.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:45.480
And the simplest set of
reactions which do the job we

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.160
can exemplify here by the
oxidation of carbon monoxide.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.400
It starts by reaction with OH.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.470
That's how most stuff is
removed from the atmosphere.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:59.010
H atoms are formed in CO2.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:00.930
O2 adds to the H atom.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:02.580
HO2 is formed.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:04.460
And then depending
on whether there

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:08.640
is enough NO in the
atmosphere, HO2 reacts with NO

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:10.710
to make OH and NO2.

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:14.790
Or in an other cycle,
HO2 can react with ozone

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:17.760
to make OH and 2O2.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:21.330
If you go to this cycle, the
NO2, which is formed here,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:25.350
is very quickly dissociated
in NO and O atoms.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:30.060
And the O atoms recombine
with oxygen to make ozone.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:31.860
The net result of
these reactions

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.880
are that CO is oxidized
with two oxygen molecules

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.610
into CO2 and ozone.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.940
If there is too little
NO in the atmosphere,

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:45.990
then instead CO is oxidized
with CO2, but ozone is consumed.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:48.670
And much depends on the amount
of NO in the atmosphere,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.900
whether ozone is
produced or destroyed.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:55.380
So basically, the borderline
is defined by the ratio

00:16:55.380 --> 00:16:57.300
between NO and ozone.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.290
And the rate constants
for various reactions

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:00.740
enter into this.

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:05.250
One can generally say that for
NO, mixing ratio is somewhere

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:08.970
greater than 10 to the
minus 11 in the atmosphere,

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:09.980
ozone is produced.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:12.680
Otherwise it can be destroyed.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:15.810
It's interesting to
look at these reactions.

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:19.800
In these photochemical reaction,
we can consider carbon monoxide

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.619
as a fuel.

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:23.970
It's a low
temperature combustion

00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:26.510
of carbon monoxide we
are really looking at,

00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:33.390
which is sort of the incinerated
by the presence of photons

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:35.350
in the atmosphere.

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:36.480
CO is the fuel.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:41.130
And we see also that we have
lots of catalysts being active

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:44.130
in the production or the
destruction of ozone.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:48.150
OH, HO2, NO, and
NO2, they all act as

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:51.330
catalysts in these reactions.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:53.930
Now this is the simplest
set of reactions

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:57.530
which ozone is formed,
photochemically and sort

00:17:57.530 --> 00:17:59.680
of smog type reactions.

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:02.340
The next simplest case
is methane oxidation,

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:05.960
which you see already many
more reactions entering

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:08.510
into the scheme.

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:13.010
But also methane is everywhere,
like carbon monoxide.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:15.920
And if there's just enough
NO in the atmosphere,

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:17.960
then ozone is form.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:22.910
And of course, if you go
to more complex molecules,

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:28.370
like isoprene, we get about
1,000 reactions to consider.

00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:32.360
And just if there's enough
NO in the atmosphere ozone

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:35.300
is formed in the same way
as during the oxidation

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:36.300
of carbon monoxide.

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:45.710
Now NO is increasing in the
atmosphere and especially

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:47.960
because of emissions
at mid-latitudes

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:51.110
and also in the
tropics by biomass

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:54.320
burning, about which
I will speak much more

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.930
a little later in the talk.

00:18:56.930 --> 00:19:00.210
With increased emissions
of NO in the atmosphere,

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.080
especially in
industrial regions,

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:06.980
and also increasing
concentrations of, say,

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:11.660
for instance, methane in the
atmosphere, one of the fuels,

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:14.840
and also carbon monoxide,
one would expect

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.620
that ozone should have gone up.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:21.120
And this is indeed observed.

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.820
There are, for instance, records
of ozone, ground level ozone,

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:31.370
going back to the end of
the last century in a suburb

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:34.310
of Paris, Montsouris.

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.340
And these are data
which have been checked

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:40.730
by Dieter Klein, his
colleagues, and seem

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:45.200
to indicate good quality
measurements of ozone

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:45.830
at that time.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:48.800
At that time, about
10 nanomole per mole

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:52.400
were the average
ozone concentrations

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:54.170
which were measured.

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:57.680
And nowadays, in
environments like this

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:00.680
there's just much more
zone, about the effect

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.220
of two or three higher.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:08.160
Also, in the '20s and
'30s, optical measurements

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:14.180
were performed, very often
in the Alp regions in Europe.

00:20:14.180 --> 00:20:16.820
And the typical concentrations
of mixing ratios,

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:19.030
which were measured
at that time,

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:22.820
were of the order of,
say, 20 nanomole per mole.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:26.960
And what is measured typically
nowadays is about 2 times more,

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:28.070
even 3 times more.

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:36.760
And also at station in southern
Germany, Hohenpeissenberg,

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:40.370
we see in the troposphere,
a substantial increase

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:43.770
from data taken in 1968--

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:45.680
these are the annual averages--

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.330
and in 1989.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:51.380
So 20 years progress in
ozone formation here.

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:59.540
This increase in
ozone seems to have

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:03.830
stopped at the
Hohenpeissenberg station.

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:09.260
You see that in the later
years things have leveled off.

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:11.660
Maybe there's even
a slight decrease.

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:13.100
This may have to
do with the fact

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:16.490
that nitrogen oxides are
being regulated in Europe,

00:21:16.490 --> 00:21:20.450
so that the maximum
ozone in Hohenpeissenberg

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:24.050
may have been reached though
by the end of the '80s.

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:25.700
Of course, it's
also possible that

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:29.750
some meteorological factors have
played a role in this curve.

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:34.140
We have to further analyze that.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:37.880
Well, also, models
can be developed.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:41.880
And when that is done,
the simplest model

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:45.110
we developed in Mainz
about 10 years ago.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:50.120
There are much more advanced
models now being used.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.280
But I think still
the principles are

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:55.200
what is happening can be
explained with the simple model

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:56.720
we developed.

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:00.560
Also, initially with the help
of Reginald Newell, the model

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:05.780
of transport in chemistry
in the atmosphere,

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:11.210
we basically divide up the
Earth in longitude and latitude

00:22:11.210 --> 00:22:14.680
blocks and high
blocks and then take

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:18.890
meteorological observations,
monthly mean wind speeds.

00:22:18.890 --> 00:22:23.270
And also a fudge factor, the
added diffusion coefficients

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:24.470
were introduced.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.100
And if you don't add
chemistry to the model,

00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:33.746
in this case, carbon monoxide
and methane chemistry,

00:22:33.746 --> 00:22:40.840
and also at the ground boundary
layer values of carbon monoxide

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:44.640
and methane, then one
can start playing games.

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:48.500
But one has to consider is that
NO is put into the atmosphere

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.900
by lightning.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:55.220
That's the same, let's
say, between industrial and

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:57.440
pre-industrial times.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:03.170
But for industrial
times, of course,

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.030
we have lots of
inputs of nitrogen

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:09.490
oxide in the industrial world.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:17.750
And this is then also
assigned to the model.

00:23:17.750 --> 00:23:21.080
And then if one calculates
how much ozone this model then

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:27.840
produces, one gets in the
case of the pre-industrial

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:31.770
atmosphere-- and this is
for the summer in July,

00:23:31.770 --> 00:23:36.270
pre-industrial atmosphere--
you see a picture which--

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:38.970
well, it's bluish
almost everywhere

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:47.070
with a few exceptions,
roughly of the order of 10,

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:51.780
15 parts per billion or nanomole
per mole near the Earth's

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.460
surface for the July case.

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.650
You compare that with
the present situation,

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:01.550
and you get a much
more colorful figure.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:06.990
See you get much enhanced
ozone concentrations, downwind

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:10.190
of the main pollution sources.

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:13.770
So an increase by
roughly effect of 2 or 3

00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:15.760
compared to
pre-industrial situation.

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:24.250
So the models basically show
the same as the observations.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:32.150
If one looks at the budget
of ozone in the troposphere,

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:37.390
we have a number of reactions
in which ozone is formed.

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:41.090
HO2 plus NO O and
CH3O2 plus NO are

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:44.480
the reactions in the carbon
monoxide methane oxidation

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:45.560
schemes.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:47.780
Any time these
reactions take place,

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:50.720
basically an ozone
molecule is formed.

00:24:50.720 --> 00:24:54.300
And you see here on the
global you see two numbers.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:59.510
One of these numbers
expresses the amount

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.120
of ozone produced
in the troposphere

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:07.460
globally in units of 10
to the 12 mole per year.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.850
And the left number
is calculated

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:12.380
for the present atmosphere.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:14.660
And the right
number is calculated

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:17.710
for the pre-industrial
atmosphere.

00:25:17.710 --> 00:25:21.500
And you see the ozone
forming reactions.

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:24.080
You see also O single D
plus water vapor, which

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:27.230
is a loss reaction for ozone.

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:33.030
There is some reaction of ozone
with HO2 and partially with OH

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.390
is coming in here.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:40.290
If you look at the chemical
terms, which are shown here,

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:44.420
you see that the
chemical terms certainly

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:46.700
for the industrial
atmosphere are much larger

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:50.510
than the flux of ozone
downwards from the stratosphere

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:52.760
and also larger than
the destruction of ozone

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:54.200
at the ground.

00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:59.720
This clearly shows that
ozone is formed and destroyed

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.360
in the troposphere
in amounts, which

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:05.510
are larger than the influx
from the stratosphere.

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:09.350
And interestingly,
this was also already

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.500
for the preindustrial
atmosphere.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:14.900
Although the emissions
of NO were smaller,

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:21.590
still, the chemical terms were
more important than the flux

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:23.540
from the stratosphere.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:26.120
So the internal budget of
ozone in the troposphere

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:32.390
is regulated by in situ
chemical processes.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:35.420
And this is, of course, a big
change from the initial belief

00:26:35.420 --> 00:26:44.650
that ozone would be a rather
inert gas in the troposphere.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:46.520
It's, however,
important to point out

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:52.010
that these reactions which
produce and destroy ozone

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.320
would never start
if there would not

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:57.120
be some influx from
the stratosphere

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:58.020
into the troposphere.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:00.680
We have here an
autocatalytic system

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:04.950
in which you need some flux
of ozone to get it started.

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:08.380
But then you actually
produce internally much more

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:13.430
or destroy much more
than the initial flux.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:19.970
Also, we did some
analysis of this.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:22.710
If you look at the
ozone in the atmosphere,

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:26.510
although the internal cycle
is so important, still

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:29.660
for the total amount of
ozone in the troposphere,

00:27:29.660 --> 00:27:34.090
the stratospheric part
is of large importance.

00:27:34.090 --> 00:27:37.130
In the upper part of the
troposphere, most of the ozone

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:42.500
you find there still has an
origin from the stratosphere.

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:48.500
And that's a part, which is
important for climate studies.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.910
Well, I'm getting
to the tropics.

00:27:52.910 --> 00:27:55.550
Before that, briefly
show you this figure.

00:27:55.550 --> 00:27:58.190
This is basically
the distribution

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:04.970
of ozone, total ozone, as a
function of latitude and time

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:06.150
of the year.

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:08.720
It's only average values.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:11.630
And what it shows is basically--

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:13.130
this was, by the
way, a figure which

00:28:13.130 --> 00:28:15.440
was made before the
ozone hole developed.

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:17.750
So this is a classic figure.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:22.850
But in general what we
see in this figure is

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:26.210
that in the tropics
there is basically always

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:29.690
some sort of an ozone
hole, a natural ozone hole.

00:28:29.690 --> 00:28:32.970
Minimum amount of total
ozone is in the tropics,

00:28:32.970 --> 00:28:36.680
which has to do with the height
of the tropo force, which

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:39.510
is much higher 18
kilometers in the tropics

00:28:39.510 --> 00:28:43.290
than at high latitudes.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:46.520
So most ultraviolet
radiation-- by far,

00:28:46.520 --> 00:28:48.650
most ultraviolet
radiation comes down

00:28:48.650 --> 00:28:51.350
to the surface in the tropics.

00:28:51.350 --> 00:28:55.080
And in the tropics you find
also in general a maximum

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:57.140
in water vapor concentration.

00:28:57.140 --> 00:29:02.180
So you would expect the
highest production of OH

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:05.660
and the highest OH
concentrations in the tropics.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:11.600
And although we have very few
measurements to confirm that,

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:15.920
if we rely on model calculation,
we see this clearly.

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:24.320
Here is depicted the
concentration of OH radicals

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:28.280
in the troposphere as
a function of latitude.

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:31.010
Southern hemisphere,
northern hemisphere here.

00:29:31.010 --> 00:29:36.530
And then also height and in
units of 10 to 6 molecules

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:39.120
per cubic centimeter.

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:41.010
So we have a maximum here.

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:43.280
That means that
many gases, which

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:48.200
have lifetimes, say, of the
order of a few months or more,

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:51.320
are mainly destroyed
in the tropics.

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:55.760
Examples of such gases
are carbon monoxide--

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:58.550
here also the destruction
rates depicted

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:00.320
as a function of latitude--

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:02.610
and methane.

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:08.010
So this is where the OH
radical is the most active

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:13.330
and most of the cleaning of
the atmosphere is taking place.

00:30:13.330 --> 00:30:22.540
The calculations with which
these calculations can

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:28.810
be checked, and earlier we
had a wonderful chemical

00:30:28.810 --> 00:30:34.410
product, which was
measured around the world,

00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:37.597
for instance, by Ron
Prinn and his colleagues.

00:30:37.597 --> 00:30:39.430
And what is shown here
is methyl chloroform.

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:43.000
Methyl chloroform is a compound
which basically is only

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.220
produced by the
chemical industry

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:46.910
and it's mainly
destroyed by reaction

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:49.470
with OH in the atmosphere.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:54.580
And one can compare at various
stations around the world

00:30:54.580 --> 00:31:01.630
the calculated and observed
mixing ratios of methyl

00:31:01.630 --> 00:31:02.980
chloroform in the atmosphere.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:04.900
And if you look at
these curves, you

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:06.220
can hardly distinguish them.

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:09.160
This model, as it was
developed about 10 years ago,

00:31:09.160 --> 00:31:11.240
gave roughly the right results.

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:15.970
So the OH concentration
cross-section,

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.820
which I showed you before,
cannot be too far off from

00:31:18.820 --> 00:31:20.716
reality.

00:31:20.716 --> 00:31:24.970
And this is very interesting
because I repeat this now

00:31:24.970 --> 00:31:29.410
here basically the
curve I have in color,

00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.200
indicating the breakdown
of carbon monoxide

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:33.880
by reaction with OH.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:37.470
And methane reaction with OH
in the industrial atmosphere

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:38.325
is this figure.

00:31:38.325 --> 00:31:42.550
And you can do the same thing in
the pre-industrial atmosphere.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:46.870
And you can say
that this represents

00:31:46.870 --> 00:31:49.270
the destruction of methane
and carbon monoxide

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:52.570
must, of course, be balanced
by input into the atmosphere.

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:56.900
So you can say that the natural
sources for carbon monoxide

00:31:56.900 --> 00:32:01.570
and methane are given by
this lower figure here.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:07.400
And of the industrial
atmosphere is given here.

00:32:07.400 --> 00:32:09.240
So here we've the
natural sources.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:13.410
Here we have sum of natural
plus anthropogenic sources.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:15.770
The difference is the
anthropogenic source.

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:19.810
So you can use these sort
of model calculations

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:25.530
to derive budget of
methane in the atmosphere.

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:31.240
And what one finds
then is that at present

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:38.260
of the order of 630
million tons of methane

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:45.700
is used in current atmosphere.

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:49.210
In the pre-industrial, the
natural source of methane

00:32:49.210 --> 00:32:51.190
is of the order of 270.

00:32:51.190 --> 00:32:54.250
And the difference is, say, 360.

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:57.790
So what this basically
shows is that an input

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:01.750
by human activities of
methane in the atmosphere

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:05.920
is probably somewhat larger than
the natural source of methane

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:07.600
in the atmosphere.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:10.210
The anthropogenic sources
of methane in the atmosphere

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:11.980
are given here.

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:15.210
The individual
amounts of are still

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:17.620
not answered in many cases.

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:21.990
But at least we know roughly
what the sum of these sources

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:22.490
should be.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:28.340
And you see here animals
play a large role.

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:31.420
Anaerobic fermentation
is a large source

00:33:31.420 --> 00:33:33.610
for methane in the atmosphere.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:41.770
So ruminants and animal
waste fermentation

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:44.275
is a large source for
methane in the atmosphere.

00:33:44.275 --> 00:33:48.880
We have rice fields,
biomass burning, landfills,

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:53.650
and natural gas and
oil industry leaks.

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:57.100
It's interesting to see how
important the animals are.

00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:59.440
And I don't know whether you
ever have thought about it,

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:08.860
but say a typical family of
five people, owns 1 point

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:11.856
1/4 of a cow.

00:34:11.856 --> 00:34:14.230
So somewhere in
the world there are

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:18.540
lots of cows supplying us here
with cheese and meat and so on.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:24.520
And we have others here, so
of the order of 10 chicken

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:26.350
just supplying us with meat.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:31.780
What I showed you
before is already

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:33.940
an indication, a strong
indication, how important

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:36.969
the tropics are in all of this.

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:41.860
To give you an idea about
the fundamental step forward,

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:46.449
which was made by the discovery
how important hydroxyl radicals

00:34:46.449 --> 00:34:49.870
are in cleaning
the atmosphere, I

00:34:49.870 --> 00:34:55.690
give you this. view graph
some so-called authoritative

00:34:55.690 --> 00:35:00.850
estimates, of budget estimates,
of methane and carbon

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:02.770
monoxide in the atmosphere.

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:07.180
In 1968, there was such
an analysis published.

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:11.890
And it claimed that the methane
source from natural wetlands

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:16.150
would be of the order of
1,180 million tonne of methane

00:35:16.150 --> 00:35:16.660
per year.

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:21.780
We know now that the real
number is of the order of 270.

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.570
At that time, there
simply was no knowledge

00:35:24.570 --> 00:35:26.970
at all available
about how things were

00:35:26.970 --> 00:35:29.130
removed from the atmosphere.

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:31.140
Anthropogenic
emissions, they were

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:34.030
rather close to each other.

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:39.870
If you look at the CO budget,
you see here number 75 in 1968.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:43.800
And we know now that the number
here for the natural source

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:46.230
is more like 860.

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:49.650
And the anthropogenic source
was also very strongly

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:51.129
underestimated at that time.

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:00.650
Some of you may think
that the tropics

00:36:00.650 --> 00:36:03.080
in the southern
hemisphere are still

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:05.476
clean regions of the atmosphere.

00:36:05.476 --> 00:36:07.350
There's very little
industry you might think.

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:12.050
But there is a very important
human activity already taking

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:13.970
place in the tropics.

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:15.560
And that is biomass burning.

00:36:15.560 --> 00:36:19.460
Whoever has been in the
tropics during the dry season

00:36:19.460 --> 00:36:23.060
knows how polluted
the atmosphere can be.

00:36:23.060 --> 00:36:29.630
And here, we have some estimates
of the amount of carbon, which

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:34.610
is burned in the tropics
by various activities,

00:36:34.610 --> 00:36:38.740
having to do in many cases with
rather primitive agriculture.

00:36:38.740 --> 00:36:41.990
The shift in
agriculture is estimated

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:46.820
that between 1,000 to 2,000
million tonne of carbon

00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:50.960
is burned each year
in this activity.

00:36:50.960 --> 00:36:55.010
Permanent deforestation,
500 to 1,400.

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:59.200
Savanna fires 400 to 2,000.

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:02.810
Fire wood burning is
better known, 300 to 600.

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:09.080
And agricultural waste
burning, say, 500 to 800 homes.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:11.540
See these numbers
are very uncertain.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:18.890
But they add up to roughly
about 3 to 7 times 10

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:21.170
to 15 gram of carbon per year.

00:37:21.170 --> 00:37:24.620
This is a very large
amount of biomass, which is

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:26.210
burned in the tropic each year.

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:28.370
And this is dirty burning.

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:33.450
I mean, a lot of pollutants
are coming into the atmosphere.

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:36.740
So this is actually what
is exposed to the fire.

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:41.160
This is what actually is
burned, between 2 and 5 times 10

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:44.120
to the 15 gram of
carbon per year.

00:37:44.120 --> 00:37:50.310
And that stuff in that burning,
stuff gets into the atmosphere.

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:52.140
And basically, all
the molecules we

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:55.110
know which are contributing
to photochemical

00:37:55.110 --> 00:38:00.960
smog in our part
of the world also

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:05.000
are admitted by biomass
burning in the atmosphere.

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:07.530
In Mainz we have set
up a little laboratory

00:38:07.530 --> 00:38:10.829
in which we can burn
materials from different parts

00:38:10.829 --> 00:38:11.370
of the world.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:15.480
And then we look at the
emissions from these materials.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:23.130
And we find typically we have
the temperature of the fires.

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:26.270
We have the flaming phase and
the smoldering phase here.

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:28.360
And together,
depending on whether we

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:32.110
are on the flaming phase or the
smoldering phase of the fire,

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:33.840
you get different
kinds of pollutants

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:37.080
in the atmosphere, like carbon
dioxide, carbon monoxide,

00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:41.520
methane, and many other
hydrocarbons, methyl chloride

00:38:41.520 --> 00:38:42.030
and so on.

00:38:42.030 --> 00:38:49.500
So a rich menu of compounds are
released into the atmosphere

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.570
by biomass burning
in the tropics.

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:57.860
And this is clearly affecting
the level of pollution gases

00:38:57.860 --> 00:39:01.020
or background gases
in the tropics,

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:06.300
and not only gases, but also
soot and smoke particles.

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:09.150
Here we have data from Cuiaba.

00:39:09.150 --> 00:39:13.350
This is a station in
Mato Grosso in Brazil.

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:16.650
And you see maximum
concentrations

00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:20.610
of aerosol in the
atmosphere, coinciding

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:22.590
with the time when
biomass burning is

00:39:22.590 --> 00:39:24.630
taking place in Brazil.

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:30.570
Now, these biomass burning
produces, as I said,

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:36.000
the same compounds,
which basically also lead

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:38.609
to photochemical smoke
in our part of the world.

00:39:38.609 --> 00:39:39.900
But there's one big difference.

00:39:39.900 --> 00:39:43.620
In the tropics this
is a rural phenomenon.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:47.550
In our part of the world, it's
mainly an urban phenomenon.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:49.340
So this is covering
much larger areas.

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:55.550
And we see the results
of that they clearly.

00:39:55.550 --> 00:40:02.270
We are going from this station
in Mato Grosso in the 1988.

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:05.690
In the month of
September for about half

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:09.590
of the time, half of the days
in the month of September,

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:13.790
the ozone levels,
80 PPB of ozone

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:18.980
was exceeded during the month of
September, the time of biomass

00:40:18.980 --> 00:40:20.735
burning in Brazil.

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:25.670
Now this is different
from year to year.

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:28.865
Some years it's just very bad.

00:40:28.865 --> 00:40:30.530
Other years it may be better.

00:40:30.530 --> 00:40:35.590
This depends on
meteorological conditions.

00:40:35.590 --> 00:40:42.170
And it's not only
happening in India Brazil,

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:49.150
but also in, say, Zimbabwe
in southern part of Africa.

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:55.360
What you see here are
profiles of smoke particles

00:40:55.360 --> 00:40:58.610
of carbon monoxide,
carbon dioxide, and ozone,

00:40:58.610 --> 00:41:03.250
which were measured
by a research team

00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:08.770
from our Institute, Andy Andre.

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:12.450
You see these maxima
in concentrations.

00:41:12.450 --> 00:41:16.150
And these are the result
of biomass burning, which

00:41:16.150 --> 00:41:19.420
doesn't taken place, of
course, at these altitudes

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:24.310
at that time, but had happened
somewhere else near the ground.

00:41:24.310 --> 00:41:27.820
And then the air was lifted
to its high altitudes,

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:31.760
where then the ozone was formed.

00:41:31.760 --> 00:41:35.500
This phenomena cannot
only been seen in situ,

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:38.240
it can also be seen from space.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:42.010
Here we have data on the
amount of carbon monoxide

00:41:42.010 --> 00:41:45.790
in the troposphere,
which were measured

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:49.240
during the wet season in
the southern hemisphere

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:52.630
and the dry season in
the southern hemisphere.

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:57.720
The blue colors mean very
little CO, relatively little CO.

00:41:57.720 --> 00:42:02.290
The red colors mean lots
of CO in the troposphere.

00:42:02.290 --> 00:42:04.710
And you see that
in October, there

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:11.140
is more carbon monoxide in the
southern hemisphere than six

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:14.800
months earlier,
basically the dry season,

00:42:14.800 --> 00:42:16.255
in the northern hemisphere.

00:42:16.255 --> 00:42:19.240
It shows you, you cannot
consider the tropics

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:20.992
in the southern
hemisphere as very clean.

00:42:24.610 --> 00:42:29.560
And this is also seen in
the ozone distribution

00:42:29.560 --> 00:42:34.540
figure, which comes
from a Jack Fischman.

00:42:34.540 --> 00:42:37.570
In the period
September to November,

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:41.890
you see that there is this
outflow, this production

00:42:41.890 --> 00:42:44.830
of ozone in the
southern hemisphere,

00:42:44.830 --> 00:42:47.230
affecting large parts of
the southern hemisphere,

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:51.760
including Australia
and Tasmania here.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:54.205
Interestingly,
this model of which

00:42:54.205 --> 00:43:01.070
I just showed you some
results before simulates that.

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:06.810
You see this tongue of
relatively high ozone values

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:08.997
extending into the
southern hemisphere.

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:15.510
So there can be a lot of ozone
in the southern hemisphere.

00:43:15.510 --> 00:43:17.410
But you can also
have circumstances

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:19.240
in which you hardly
find any ozone

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:20.710
in the southern hemisphere.

00:43:20.710 --> 00:43:23.390
And that is in regions
where basically you

00:43:23.390 --> 00:43:25.840
are far away from
the continents,

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:31.120
from the inputs of anthropogenic
and also natural hydrocarbons

00:43:31.120 --> 00:43:34.090
and nitrogen oxides
in the atmosphere.

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:40.840
In 1993, we did measurements.

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:47.410
This was an activity by the
Center for Studies of Climate

00:43:47.410 --> 00:43:55.360
and Clouds at the
Scripps Institution

00:43:55.360 --> 00:44:00.900
of Oceanography in collaboration
with Dieter Klein in Germany.

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:06.780
A cruise was made from
Honiara to Solomon Islands

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:09.310
to Christmas Island.

00:44:09.310 --> 00:44:11.920
And during this
cruise, balloons were

00:44:11.920 --> 00:44:14.380
launched into the atmosphere.

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:16.870
And ozone was measured.

00:44:16.870 --> 00:44:22.000
And what was often mentioned
was ozone profiles which

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:26.150
looked like this, close to the
tropopause, hardly any ozone,

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:28.210
it was gone.

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:30.360
And also in the
lower troposphere,

00:44:30.360 --> 00:44:34.060
rather low concentrations
of ozone in the troposphere.

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:35.770
This was expected.

00:44:35.770 --> 00:44:38.260
But that we could end
up with such low ozone

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:40.610
values in the upper
troposphere, that was new.

00:44:40.610 --> 00:44:44.020
And it was not only
measured on one profile.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:49.890
But it was measured on several
profiles, several locations.

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:54.000
And this is shown in
these panels here.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:58.000
You see very often
situations in which

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:04.720
ozone goes down to almost
zero here and here again, etc.

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:06.940
So this was a big surprise.

00:45:06.940 --> 00:45:10.000
And I'm not sure that we
understand very well why

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:12.680
this is happening.

00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:18.100
So if we consider the enormous
importance of the hydroxyl

00:45:18.100 --> 00:45:20.260
radical in keeping
the atmosphere clean,

00:45:20.260 --> 00:45:23.500
and we realize how
important ozone

00:45:23.500 --> 00:45:26.860
is as a precursor
for hydroxyl radicals

00:45:26.860 --> 00:45:30.160
and how hydroxyl
radicals are mainly

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:33.910
located in the troposphere,
we must, of course,

00:45:33.910 --> 00:45:41.430
wonder about the ozone
distribution in the tropics.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:43.900
We cannot say that this
distribution of ozone

00:45:43.900 --> 00:45:47.020
in the tropics is a very
regular distribution,

00:45:47.020 --> 00:45:48.460
because it's highly variable.

00:45:48.460 --> 00:45:52.040
We can have situations
with very little ozone.

00:45:52.040 --> 00:45:55.370
Or we can have situations
with much more ozone, whenever

00:45:55.370 --> 00:45:59.710
we are under the influence of
biomass burning for instance.

00:45:59.710 --> 00:46:04.090
So in order to understand
the atmospheric chemistry,

00:46:04.090 --> 00:46:07.060
we have to understand what
is happening in the tropics.

00:46:07.060 --> 00:46:10.180
And we have to understand
the distribution of ozone

00:46:10.180 --> 00:46:10.900
in the tropics.

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:14.440
And unfortunately, if
you look at the stations

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:17.780
where the measurements in the
tropics are occasionally made,

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:19.120
they are very few.

00:46:19.120 --> 00:46:24.490
Just far too few to set up a
good basis, quantitative basis

00:46:24.490 --> 00:46:28.430
for our studies.

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:32.340
So one of the
messages of my talk

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:36.380
is that we have to improve on
that situation very strongly.

00:46:36.380 --> 00:46:39.980
I would like to show you
at the end the few most

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:42.730
recent results.

00:46:42.730 --> 00:46:45.790
I showed you before
this figure, which

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:50.860
indicates the oxidation
schematically of isoprene

00:46:50.860 --> 00:46:51.630
in the atmosphere.

00:46:51.630 --> 00:46:55.000
Isoprene is emitted
from tropical forests

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:57.820
in large amounts
in the atmosphere.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:01.251
And during its oxidation very
interesting chemical things

00:47:01.251 --> 00:47:01.750
happen.

00:47:04.540 --> 00:47:06.020
And these were measured by us.

00:47:06.020 --> 00:47:10.450
In the beginning of last
year, we had an expedition.

00:47:10.450 --> 00:47:16.660
In Suriname, this is
the former Dutch Guiana,

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:22.510
where we on an aircraft
measured in situ

00:47:22.510 --> 00:47:26.170
the distribution of
isoprene and products,

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:28.630
which are derived from
isoprene in the atmosphere,

00:47:28.630 --> 00:47:32.410
with a new instrument, the
proton transfer reaction mass

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:34.360
spectrometer.

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:37.550
And whenever we were
in the boundary layer--

00:47:37.550 --> 00:47:40.800
here we have the flight
route of the plane--

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:44.320
whenever we were in boundary
layer, we saw isoprene,

00:47:44.320 --> 00:47:53.680
and we saw methyl vinyl ketone
and also hydroxy hydroperoxide,

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:56.140
which derives from isoprene.

00:47:56.140 --> 00:47:57.110
So they're all there.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:48:00.190
And the effect, the
model which we developed

00:48:00.190 --> 00:48:02.080
gave roughly the same results.

00:48:02.080 --> 00:48:05.920
The Lagrangian model fed
by emission of isoprene

00:48:05.920 --> 00:48:10.030
from the forest gave about the
amounts which were mentioned.

00:48:10.030 --> 00:48:13.660
In fact, it was so much isoprene
was emitted in the atmosphere

00:48:13.660 --> 00:48:17.740
that it ditrated hydroxyl
radicals to a large degree.

00:48:17.740 --> 00:48:21.580
So the average OH
concentration, average

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:25.180
over 24 hours in the boundary
layer over the forest, which

00:48:25.180 --> 00:48:28.540
calculated was only 2
times 10 to the 5 molecules

00:48:28.540 --> 00:48:29.735
per cubic centimeter.

00:48:29.735 --> 00:48:32.790
It's a very low amount
for a tropical region.

00:48:35.670 --> 00:48:40.860
We saw other stuff
in surprisingly high

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:43.060
concentrations.

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:48.360
One compound which is of
substantial importance is--

00:48:48.360 --> 00:48:49.930
of course, carbon
monoxide we saw,

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:55.140
but also we saw
acetone mixing ratios

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:58.840
of the order of 2 nanomole
per mole and even more.

00:48:58.840 --> 00:49:04.910
And acetone it turns out-- this
is research of the last 5 to 10

00:49:04.910 --> 00:49:05.710
years--

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:08.250
is an important source
for hydroxyl radicals

00:49:08.250 --> 00:49:09.510
at high altitudes.

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:13.290
So it's definitely formed
in the tropics in the forest

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:15.450
regions in large amounts.

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:18.190
And we also saw acitonitrile.

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:20.190
This is a product
of biomass burning

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:24.780
in the tropics and
elsewhere of course.

00:49:24.780 --> 00:49:35.481
Finally some results from a more
recent campaign in the tropics.

00:49:35.481 --> 00:49:40.050
The Indo X campaign
which was conducted

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:45.360
last February, March in
the Indian Ocean region

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:48.660
with a base in the Maldives.

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:54.090
During this experiment, which
occupied some 150 scientists

00:49:54.090 --> 00:49:57.170
from all around the
world, including India,

00:49:57.170 --> 00:50:01.200
during this experiment,
many measurements

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:03.480
were made from
ships, from aircraft,

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.550
and also ground based.

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:07.590
The idea about
this experiment was

00:50:07.590 --> 00:50:10.230
to see whether the
pollution, which

00:50:10.230 --> 00:50:12.300
is produced over the
Indian continent,

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:15.060
in general south
and southeast Asia,

00:50:15.060 --> 00:50:19.680
whether it could reach
the Indian Ocean region.

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:22.320
So we went out there.

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:29.091
And we also went south of the
ITCZ, Intertropical Convergence

00:50:29.091 --> 00:50:29.590
Zone.

00:50:29.590 --> 00:50:33.870
And you see a nice atmosphere,
not too dirty, rather clean.

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:41.190
But then, mostly we were
south of the Intertropical

00:50:41.190 --> 00:50:43.050
Convergence Zone.

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:45.090
And there it looked like this.

00:50:45.090 --> 00:50:47.490
It was dirty over
very large regions.

00:50:47.490 --> 00:50:51.400
Down to the ITCZ, the
Indian Ocean region

00:50:51.400 --> 00:50:52.316
was highly polluted.

00:50:56.044 --> 00:50:58.440
It was polluted
to such an extent

00:50:58.440 --> 00:51:05.050
that-- and mostly what
you see is the particles,

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:12.640
the absorption optical
depth of the particles

00:51:12.640 --> 00:51:17.870
could be of the order of 0.04.

00:51:17.870 --> 00:51:21.520
So this rather dark stuff
which was out there.

00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:24.120
And the effect on
the radiation balance

00:51:24.120 --> 00:51:28.210
of a substantial region over the
Indian Ocean is considerable.

00:51:28.210 --> 00:51:35.590
So some estimates indicate
that under these conditions

00:51:35.590 --> 00:51:38.680
about 20% of the solar
radiation, which normally

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:42.940
should reach the surface of
the ocean didn't arrive there.

00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:44.800
It was either absorbed
in the atmosphere

00:51:44.800 --> 00:51:47.240
or backscattered to space.

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:51.680
So this was a rather
shocking experience for us.

00:51:51.680 --> 00:51:55.660
And we were curious
to find something.

00:51:55.660 --> 00:51:58.610
We were a little worried that
maybe we don't find anything,

00:51:58.610 --> 00:52:01.240
because it's so far away
from the pollution source.

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:03.210
But that we would
find such a situation

00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:07.660
was a big surprise
for everybody for us.

00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:13.720
So that shows that we
have to start already now

00:52:13.720 --> 00:52:17.320
worrying about what is
happening in the tropics

00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:20.870
and in the developing world.

00:52:20.870 --> 00:52:23.170
I mean, this is where
most of the action

00:52:23.170 --> 00:52:25.260
will be in the future.

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:28.600
And this is my pitch, of course.

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:31.570
I mean, that's where
much more effort

00:52:31.570 --> 00:52:34.140
should go into this research.

00:52:34.140 --> 00:52:38.800
We have to find out what is
happening in the tropics.

00:52:38.800 --> 00:52:42.310
And we have so few
data at the moment.

00:52:42.310 --> 00:52:46.590
So I hope that with your
support and a little propaganda,

00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:49.479
we can do much more of
these kinds of experiments.

00:52:49.479 --> 00:52:50.770
I thank you for your attention.

00:52:50.770 --> 00:52:53.827
And I hope there's still
allowed some questions.

00:52:53.827 --> 00:52:57.306
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:06.749 --> 00:53:08.240
MODERATOR: Questions
and comments?

00:53:14.204 --> 00:53:16.192
AUDIENCE: Where
did this pollution

00:53:16.192 --> 00:53:18.425
come from that you showed us.

00:53:18.425 --> 00:53:22.330
CRUTZEN: From India and
Southeast Asia and probably

00:53:22.330 --> 00:53:23.020
also China.

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:36.200
It's a huge population behind
it, some 2,000 million people.

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:38.150
AUDIENCE: Fuel burning?

00:53:38.150 --> 00:53:39.530
CRUTZEN: Yes, it's wood burning.

00:53:39.530 --> 00:53:41.330
I think that's part of it.

00:53:41.330 --> 00:53:44.840
But there's also lots
of dirty burning.

00:53:47.580 --> 00:53:52.540
We have to find out actually
where it all came from.

00:53:55.160 --> 00:54:01.240
But it contained a large
amount of absorbing aerosol.

00:54:01.240 --> 00:54:05.405
So it's different
from sulfate aerosol.

00:54:05.405 --> 00:54:09.117
AUDIENCE: Paul, there are
actually some HOx measurements

00:54:09.117 --> 00:54:15.305
in the tropical troposphere last
time I was reading about it.

00:54:15.305 --> 00:54:16.750
CRUTZEN: Which measurements?

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:17.910
AUDIENCE: HOx.

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:18.750
CRUTZEN: HOx.

00:54:18.750 --> 00:54:22.170
Oh, I haven't looked into that.

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:28.370
When I said the model
agreed rather well

00:54:28.370 --> 00:54:31.620
with what was observed, this
was for the boundary layer.

00:54:31.620 --> 00:54:35.620
You referred to the
measurements in Suriname?

00:54:35.620 --> 00:54:38.910
AUDIENCE: No, I'm talking
about the HOx measurement

00:54:38.910 --> 00:54:41.730
from here too.

00:54:41.730 --> 00:54:45.590
CRUTZEN: Yeah, well, I have
not developed any model

00:54:45.590 --> 00:54:48.790
calculations for that.

00:54:48.790 --> 00:54:54.520
AUDIENCE: Do you expect similar
situations with biomass burning

00:54:54.520 --> 00:54:58.780
in Africa and the Amazon.

00:54:58.780 --> 00:55:01.309
Is there any evidence
for the similar extension

00:55:01.309 --> 00:55:04.414
of the pollution?

00:55:04.414 --> 00:55:05.330
CRUTZEN: I don't know.

00:55:05.330 --> 00:55:08.240
I mean, we have to go out there.

00:55:08.240 --> 00:55:11.660
I mean, of course,
far fewer people

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:15.970
living in these other
parts of the world.

00:55:15.970 --> 00:55:20.250
I think we should
follow up on this.

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:22.900
AUDIENCE: If you
look at the tropics,

00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:28.445
and you look at this large scale
perturbations to the chemical

00:55:28.445 --> 00:55:32.810
composition to
[INAUDIBLE] are you more

00:55:32.810 --> 00:55:36.527
worried about the impact of
the chemicals per se or are

00:55:36.527 --> 00:55:43.070
you worried [INAUDIBLE]
between chemicals [INAUDIBLE]

00:55:43.070 --> 00:55:46.640
CRUTZEN: The latter I am more.

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:49.460
Worried about, yes,
that's right word.

00:55:49.460 --> 00:55:51.020
But on the other
hand, of course,

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:54.230
it's also highly,
awfully interesting

00:55:54.230 --> 00:55:55.840
finding out more about this.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:55:57.950
But what does this all mean?

00:55:57.950 --> 00:56:02.524
I mean when suddenly 20%
of the solar radiation

00:56:02.524 --> 00:56:04.190
is not appearing at
the Earth's surface.

00:56:04.190 --> 00:56:05.990
It must do something.

00:56:05.990 --> 00:56:07.040
Maybe not immediately.

00:56:07.040 --> 00:56:11.830
Maybe we are in the process
of building up some something.

00:56:15.076 --> 00:56:17.391
MOLINA: We made a
question [INAUDIBLE]

00:56:17.391 --> 00:56:21.575
you would need a budget
comparing [INAUDIBLE]

00:56:21.575 --> 00:56:22.594
What about OH?

00:56:22.594 --> 00:56:26.135
What do the models--

00:56:26.135 --> 00:56:29.870
CRUTZEN: Well, for OH, I
would not trust the models.

00:56:29.870 --> 00:56:33.520
I mean, we roughly are
in the right ballpark.

00:56:33.520 --> 00:56:36.430
And I mean, we were very lucky
when we got these results.

00:56:36.430 --> 00:56:40.340
And with additional
chemistry, we

00:56:40.340 --> 00:56:43.070
have added to the
model in the meanwhile.

00:56:43.070 --> 00:56:45.170
We actually get somewhat
different results,

00:56:45.170 --> 00:56:50.600
which still look quite good, but
not so good as indicated here.

00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:54.200
So it means that in the
current models, which

00:56:54.200 --> 00:56:57.800
are more advanced, there's
something missing, which

00:56:57.800 --> 00:57:00.240
we did not discover
before when we had

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:01.750
much more simplified models.

00:57:01.750 --> 00:57:03.830
So this is sort
of contradictory,

00:57:03.830 --> 00:57:06.800
but this is the case.

00:57:10.632 --> 00:57:12.440
Rowland can better
answer this question

00:57:12.440 --> 00:57:14.790
of what is happening to OH.

00:57:14.790 --> 00:57:18.230
You cannot say in general
OH will grow or decrease

00:57:18.230 --> 00:57:19.760
by so and so many percent.

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:21.710
It would be parts
of the world where

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:24.230
hydroxyl will
increase wherever you

00:57:24.230 --> 00:57:25.480
put more NO in the atmosphere.

00:57:25.480 --> 00:57:27.200
And the other parts
of the world where

00:57:27.200 --> 00:57:30.020
it will decrease because
more carbon monoxide

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:32.150
is put into the atmosphere.

00:57:32.150 --> 00:57:35.210
And the global balance,
I don't think our models

00:57:35.210 --> 00:57:41.600
are, by far, not good enough
to derive lower balance.

00:57:41.600 --> 00:57:46.070
I think we need traces to do
that and other means will do.

00:57:49.528 --> 00:57:52.739
AUDIENCE: We published
a paper a few years ago

00:57:52.739 --> 00:57:56.444
claiming that it was
about 0 plus or minus 0.2%

00:57:56.444 --> 00:57:57.926
or 0.3% per year.

00:57:57.926 --> 00:57:59.408
So it's rather small.

00:57:59.408 --> 00:58:05.583
About all we do now to modify
that [INAUDIBLE] equals 0.

00:58:05.583 --> 00:58:07.806
CRUTZEN: Yeah.

00:58:07.806 --> 00:58:11.758
AUDIENCE: Sometimes [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:11.758 --> 00:58:14.228
CRUTZEN: Yeah.

00:58:14.228 --> 00:58:16.204
AUDIENCE: Well, I
think [INAUDIBLE]..

00:58:16.204 --> 00:58:19.168
There are really two sets
of problems connected

00:58:19.168 --> 00:58:20.650
with the less developed world.

00:58:20.650 --> 00:58:25.352
One is the pollution where
people aren't, which you just

00:58:25.352 --> 00:58:26.340
showed us.

00:58:26.340 --> 00:58:28.810
The other is the
pollution where people

00:58:28.810 --> 00:58:33.960
are in these mega cities, where
the air is even much worse when

00:58:33.960 --> 00:58:36.435
measured out in
the Indian Ocean.

00:58:36.435 --> 00:58:39.405
And it's very difficult
to get resources

00:58:39.405 --> 00:58:41.385
to study either problem.

00:58:41.385 --> 00:58:46.016
But what I wonder is to
what extent the emissions

00:58:46.016 --> 00:58:49.680
from converging and
multiplying mega cities

00:58:49.680 --> 00:58:52.488
in less the
developed world, what

00:58:52.488 --> 00:58:57.822
the magnitude of that impact
is on a regional, semi-global

00:58:57.822 --> 00:59:01.175
scale versus the story
that you told us today

00:59:01.175 --> 00:59:02.630
about biomass burning.

00:59:02.630 --> 00:59:06.200
It's not clear to me that
the two impacts [INAUDIBLE]

00:59:06.200 --> 00:59:07.640
similar magnitude.

00:59:10.530 --> 00:59:14.630
CRUTZEN: Well, the Indo
X pollution, I think,

00:59:14.630 --> 00:59:17.010
was not only biomass
burning, but it can also

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:20.890
be dirty coal burning and
all of that stuff behind it.

00:59:20.890 --> 00:59:23.670
So we simply have to find out.

00:59:23.670 --> 00:59:28.120
But I mean what will happen in
the future with the big city

00:59:28.120 --> 00:59:32.840
glomerates, I mean, with 30
million people living together,

00:59:32.840 --> 00:59:36.840
how that will change the overall
chemistry of the atmosphere

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:38.670
is a very important question.

00:59:38.670 --> 00:59:41.910
I mean, what is better, to
spread out all this pollution

00:59:41.910 --> 00:59:49.020
over a large area or put it
all together mainly big cities?

00:59:49.020 --> 00:59:55.130
You create totally different
types of aerosol, for instance.

00:59:55.130 --> 00:59:57.880
The coagulation of cities
is so much stronger.

00:59:57.880 --> 01:00:01.500
And so what the developments
of that will be--

01:00:01.500 --> 01:00:05.760
I mean, the influence of
cities even on the global scale

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:09.850
is, I think, a very interesting
and important issue.

01:00:09.850 --> 01:00:13.802
AUDIENCE: Well, let me ask
a question on the main theme

01:00:13.802 --> 01:00:17.933
that there needs to be more
studies in the chemistry.

01:00:17.933 --> 01:00:20.224
Most of the probable countries
are developing countries

01:00:20.224 --> 01:00:22.694
and they don't have
a lot of resources

01:00:22.694 --> 01:00:25.164
to do this type of work.

01:00:25.164 --> 01:00:29.610
I mean the instrumentation
and certainly [INAUDIBLE]

01:00:29.610 --> 01:00:32.080
expensive [INAUDIBLE].

01:00:32.080 --> 01:00:35.165
How many Indians were
in Indo X for example?

01:00:35.165 --> 01:00:37.040
How many Indian scientists
were [INAUDIBLE]??

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:39.270
CRUTZEN: Quite many, quite many.

01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:42.150
I mean if I would
guess now, probably

01:00:42.150 --> 01:00:46.590
of the order of 30 to 50.

01:00:46.590 --> 01:00:51.630
So they were mainly on one of
the ships, the Sagar Kanya.

01:00:51.630 --> 01:00:55.730
And really some, quite a few
of them do excellent work.

01:00:55.730 --> 01:00:59.080
So it's not a
hopeless situation.

01:00:59.080 --> 01:01:03.930
But, of course, you
raise an important point.

01:01:03.930 --> 01:01:06.630
How do you get more
of these people

01:01:06.630 --> 01:01:08.990
in developing countries
into this game?

01:01:08.990 --> 01:01:11.820
It's high technology.

01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:18.090
I mean, you can
educate people here,

01:01:18.090 --> 01:01:21.760
but do they go home to their
country and to do the research?

01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:23.974
They stay here because
when they go home,

01:01:23.974 --> 01:01:25.140
there's nothing to do there.

01:01:25.140 --> 01:01:27.430
The governments
are not interested.

01:01:27.430 --> 01:01:30.390
But there you sit at
international negotiations,

01:01:30.390 --> 01:01:33.210
and there you see
most of the scientists

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:36.600
from the developing countries
sitting in the back rows

01:01:36.600 --> 01:01:38.190
and not saying much.

01:01:38.190 --> 01:01:41.590
And so they do not
inform their governments.

01:01:41.590 --> 01:01:45.270
So you have this
terrible vicious circle

01:01:45.270 --> 01:01:48.560
of things, which in some
way, we have to break.

01:01:48.560 --> 01:01:51.591
But how we is not
clear to me either.

01:01:51.591 --> 01:01:54.380
MOLINA: I think that's question
about being a little bit

01:01:54.380 --> 01:01:57.296
better for the large
cities because there are

01:01:57.296 --> 01:01:59.726
more resources in the cities.

01:01:59.726 --> 01:02:03.700
And so there's at least more
scientists in the developing

01:02:03.700 --> 01:02:05.700
countries looking at that.

01:02:05.700 --> 01:02:08.338
Of course that doesn't mean
that more needs to be done.

01:02:08.338 --> 01:02:12.488
But as far as the rural
areas, I think at the moment,

01:02:12.488 --> 01:02:16.970
I would agree with you
very little is done.

01:02:16.970 --> 01:02:19.034
But there's also
scientific question

01:02:19.034 --> 01:02:21.364
I thought of that you raised.

01:02:21.364 --> 01:02:24.770
This difference between
spreading out the pollution

01:02:24.770 --> 01:02:28.160
or getting it in cities,
because [INAUDIBLE] and you

01:02:28.160 --> 01:02:31.555
sort of alluded to
one that the isoprene

01:02:31.555 --> 01:02:33.980
was titrated [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:33.980 --> 01:02:39.800
We can also envision [INAUDIBLE]
they need to destroy ozone.

01:02:39.800 --> 01:02:40.992
So all sorts of--

01:02:40.992 --> 01:02:42.950
CRUTZEN: And then more
efficiently removed then

01:02:42.950 --> 01:02:47.480
maybe if you put them all
together in a smaller--

01:02:47.480 --> 01:02:52.200
So what is the effect
of urbanization

01:02:52.200 --> 01:02:56.060
in the next century especially
on atmospheric chemistry

01:02:56.060 --> 01:03:00.030
is an interesting question.

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:02.840
It has its positive
and negative sides.

01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:05.450
Well, I don't think
at all, of course,

01:03:05.450 --> 01:03:10.190
how young kids will grow up in
cities with 30 million people.

01:03:10.190 --> 01:03:13.530
That's a totally
different problem.

01:03:13.530 --> 01:03:17.630
That's a social question,
which I find horrifying.

01:03:21.550 --> 01:03:25.450
MOLINA: Well, let's
thank our speaker again.

01:03:25.450 --> 01:03:29.488
And I invite you all
to the ninth floor

01:03:29.488 --> 01:03:32.380
where we have a
reception [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:32.380 --> 01:03:35.080
further celebrate
our [INAUDIBLE]..

01:03:35.080 --> 01:03:36.280
So thank you.

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:39.630
[APPLAUSE]

